CH = dict(num=12, tag="Geography · Ch 7", theme="Transport, communication and trade as the lifelines of the economy",
 title="Lifelines of National Economy",
 intro="Resources become useful only when they reach people and industries. Transport, communication and trade link producers with consumers and regions with one another, and so act as the arteries of the economy. This chapter studies roads, railways, pipelines, waterways, ports, airways, communication, trade and tourism.",
 big_questions=["Why are transport and communication called lifelines?", "How do roads, railways, pipelines, waterways and airways differ?", "What is the role of trade and tourism?"],
 blocks=[
 ("h2", "Transport"),
 ("p", "Movement of goods and services as well as people over space is called transport. The pace of development of a country depends on the production of goods and services and their movement; the means include land, water and air, and today **transport, communication and trade** are complementary."),
 ("h3", "Roadways"),
 ("ul", ["India has one of the largest road networks in the world, over 60 lakh km at present. Roads are cheaper to construct than railways, can negotiate steep slopes, link remote areas, are suitable for short distances and are important for door-to-door service.", "Roads are classified by capacity: **Golden Quadrilateral Super Highways**, National Highways, State Highways, District roads, Other roads and **Border roads**.", "The **Golden Quadrilateral** links the four metropolitan cities Delhi, Kolkata, Chennai and Mumbai by six-lane super highways; it is about 5,846 km long and is managed by the **National Highways Authority of India (NHAI)**. The **North-South Corridor** connects **Srinagar** to **Kanyakumari**, and the **East-West Corridor** connects **Silchar (Assam)** to **Porbandar (Gujarat)**.", "**National Highways** link extreme parts of the country, constructed and maintained by the **Central Public Works Department (CPWD)**; **State Highways** link state capitals with district headquarters, maintained by the state PWD; **District Roads** link district headquarters with other places; **Other roads** include rural roads under the **Pradhan Mantri Gram Sadak Yojana (PMGSY)**; **Border roads** are built by the **Border Roads Organisation (BRO, 1960)** in remote areas such as the north and north-east, to develop and secure borders.", "Limits: road density in the north-eastern states is low; roads are congested and many bridges and culverts are narrow."]),
 ("h3", "Railways"),
 ("ul", ["Railways are the principal mode of transport for freight and passengers in India; they also make it possible to conduct multifarious activities such as business, sightseeing, pilgrimage and transporting goods over long distances. The first train ran from **Mumbai to Thane in 1853**.", "The network runs on routes of about 67,000 km and is divided into zones. Railway track densities are highest in the northern plains (with extensive flat land and high population density). The Himalayan mountains, hilly terrains of the peninsula, the Thar desert and the forested tracts of Madhya Pradesh, Chhattisgarh, Odisha and Jharkhand are not favourable to railways.", "The **Konkan Railway** connecting Mumbai to Mangalore is an engineering achievement; in the Himalayas, narrow-gauge lines like Kalka-Shimla and Darjeeling help tourism."]),
 ("h3", "Pipelines"),
 ("p", "Pipelines transport crude oil, petroleum products and natural gas from oil fields to refineries and fertiliser factories; solids can be transported as slurry. Initial cost is high but running cost is minimal; it rules out transhipment losses and delays. Major pipelines: Naharkatiya (Assam) to Barauni (Bihar) through Guwahati; **Hazira-Vijaipur-Jagdishpur (HVJ)** gas pipeline; Mumbai High to Koyali (Gujarat); Salaya to Mathura."),
 ("h3", "Waterways"),
 ("p", "Waterways are the cheapest means of transport, fuel-efficient and eco-friendly, useful for heavy and bulky goods. India has about 14,500 km of navigable waterways; **National Waterways** are declared by the government."),
 ("table", ["Waterway", "Stretch"], [["NW-1", "Ganga: Prayagraj (Allahabad) to Haldia, 1,620 km"], ["NW-2", "Brahmaputra: Sadiya to Dhubri, 891 km"], ["NW-3", "West Coast Canal in Kerala: Kottapuram to Kollam, with Udyogmandal and Champakkara canals, 205 km"], ["NW-4", "Kakinada-Puducherry stretch of canals, with parts of the Godavari and Krishna rivers"]]),
 ("p", "**Major ports:** Kandla (Gujarat, a tidal port), Mumbai (a major port with a spacious natural, well-sheltered harbour), **Jawaharlal Nehru Port (Nhava Sheva)** to relieve Mumbai, Marmagao (Goa), New Mangalore, Kochi, Tuticorin, **Chennai (one of the oldest artificial ports)**, Visakhapatnam (deepest landlocked port), Paradip (Odisha) and Kolkata (riverine port) with **Haldia** built to relieve congestion."),
 ("h3", "Airways"),
 ("p", "Air travel is the fastest, most comfortable and prestigious mode of transport; it can cover difficult terrain and long distances in less time. Nationalised in 1953, the national carrier is **Air India**; **Pawan Hans** provides helicopter services to the Oil and Natural Gas Corporation and to remote areas such as the North-east, and to Jammu and Kashmir, Himachal Pradesh and Uttarakhand. Air transport is vital for the north-eastern states."),
 ("h2", "Communication"),
 ("ul", ["Personal communication (post, telegram, telephone, mobile, internet) and mass communication (radio, television, newspapers, films).", "The **Indian postal network** is the largest in the world; cards and envelopes are first class mail, and books, newspapers and periodicals are second class. Mails are carried by rail and air.", "India has one of the largest telecom networks in Asia; STD, ISD and mobile services link even rural areas. The use of **internet** has transformed communication and e-commerce."]),
 ("h2", "International Trade"),
 ("ul", ["The exchange of goods among people, states and countries is **trade**. Trade between two countries is international trade, which may take place via sea, air or land routes. **Advancement of international trade of a country is an index of its economic prosperity.**", "**Balance of trade**: the difference between exports and imports. If exports exceed imports, it is favourable; if imports exceed exports, unfavourable.", "India's exports: gems and jewellery, chemicals, engineering goods, software, textiles, agricultural goods and petroleum products; imports: petroleum and crude, pearls, precious stones, fertilisers, machinery, gold and edible oils. India's main trade partners include the USA, China, the UAE, Saudi Arabia and the European Union.", "**Software exports** have boomed since the 1990s."]),
 ("h2", "Tourism as a Trade"),
 ("ul", ["Millions of foreign tourists visit India every year, and domestic tourism is far larger. Tourism provides employment to millions directly, promotes national integration, supports local handicrafts and cultural heritage, and helps international understanding.", "Types: heritage, medical, adventure, cultural and business tourism; India is known for its **medical tourism**."]),
 ("h2", "Quick Revision: Likely Exam Questions"),
 ("qa", [
  ("Why are transport and communication lifelines?", "They link producers, markets and people and speed up development."),
  ("What is the Golden Quadrilateral?", "Six-lane highway joining Delhi, Kolkata, Chennai and Mumbai, built by NHAI."),
  ("Name the North-South and East-West corridors.", "Srinagar-Kanyakumari and Silchar-Porbandar."),
  ("What is NW-1?", "Ganga waterway from Prayagraj to Haldia."),
  ("Which port is the biggest in India?", "Mumbai."),
  ("What is balance of trade?", "Difference between value of exports and imports."),
  ("Name one advantage of pipelines.", "Low running cost and no transhipment losses."),
 ]),
 ],
 takeaways=["Transport, communication and trade are the **lifelines** of the economy.", "The **Golden Quadrilateral** and the North-South and East-West corridors are key road links.", "Railways, pipelines, waterways (**NW-1, NW-2, NW-3**) and airways each serve distinct needs.", "India's postal and telecom networks are among the largest.", "Trade balance and tourism reflect economic prosperity."])
